WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.380
Someone from, I think, the Institute of Politics at Loyola [University] called me originally.

2
00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:08.820
It might have been [Ed Redwink?] when I moved here.

3
00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:13.980
When I was in Houston, the guy I worked for . . . actually did political campaigns, and he liked them.

4
00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:18.020
And I honestly at that time, I didn’t care if someone was a Republican or a Democrat, you know.

5
00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:24.040
I just thought the idea of actually designing something and having it up was a pretty fun thing to do.

6
00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:26.140
You design a billboard, it’s up immediately.

7
00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:29.640
You take part in a TV spot and people respond to it immediately.

8
00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:35.080
And there’s polling done, and so they say, “Okay, we love the candidate.”

9
00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:38.300
Immediately he gets better polling numbers, and you think we had something to do with that.

10
00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:42.480
And so the idea of actually having that sort of power as the designer,

11
00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:49.420
when most people thought designers—and still do a lot of times think people who are designers are the ones who do sketches on the corner.

12
00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:50.220
You know, they are not.

13
00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:56.160
In fact, I had one candidate, Sidney Barthelemy, who was the mayor of New Orleans,

14
00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:01.080
didn’t know how to refer to me, and we were being paid pretty well.

15
00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:09.620
There was a team of three or four of us who were doing all of his work, and he introduced me at this huge fundraising gathering,

16
00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:19.100
people that were going to take part in the campaign, and he got to me, and he said, “This is Tom Varisco, my art guy.”
